\section{Stability}{

From crywolf 1.9.0, the names documented on this page are frozen: the
readable parameter and derived-quantity names, the columns of the
summaries and of the other results described below, and the fields of
the fitted object listed under "Readable draws and raw Stan names". They
change only in a major version, announced in the previous minor version.
New options may add names (as \code{random_det} added \code{sd_det_site}) without
changing existing ones. Raw Stan names and the fields of \code{design} and
\code{stan_data} are implementation details and are not covered. From 2.0.0
the guarantee extends to arguments, classes and the results of the
other stable functions: see \link{crywolf-api}.
}

\section{Coefficient names}{

Suffixes come from the columns of R's \code{\link[stats:model.matrix]{stats::model.matrix()}}: \code{(Intercept)}
for an intercept, the covariate name for a numeric slope, factor contrasts
such as \code{habitatforest}, and interaction columns such as \code{temp:rh}.
They refer to the covariate scale supplied to the fit; crywolf does not
automatically standardize formula covariates.\tabular{ll}{
   Name \tab Meaning and availability \cr
   \code{occ_(Intercept)}, \verb{occ_<term>} \tab Coefficients of \code{occ}, on the logit scale of occupancy probability. Always present. \cr
   \code{det_(Intercept)}, \verb{det_<term>} \tab Coefficients of \code{det}, on the logit scale of automated detection at occupied sites in the Miller design; in the Chambert design, of the true-detection component before combining true and false detections. Always present. \cr
   \code{det_y_prev} \tab Coefficient of the previous automated detection when \code{markov} is supplied. A log odds change within the same site, not a transition in occupancy. \cr
   \code{fp_(Intercept)}, \verb{fp_<term>} \tab Coefficients of \code{fp}, on the logit scale of the false-positive probability at unoccupied sites. Present when \code{fp} is not \code{NULL}. \cr
   \code{verified_(Intercept)}, \verb{verified_<term>} \tab Coefficients of \code{ver}, on the logit scale of verified-method detection at occupied sites. Present only when verified occasions enter the fit. \cr
   \code{fp_gap} \tab Positive difference \code{det_(Intercept) - fp_(Intercept)} on the logit scale. Present when \code{fp} is not \code{NULL}. It orders the reference intercepts, not all covariate-specific probabilities. \cr
   \code{sd_recorder} \tab Standard deviation of the recorder random intercept on the detection logit scale, when the recorder effect is enabled. \cr
   \code{sd_fp_site} \tab Standard deviation of the site random intercept on the false-positive logit scale, when \code{random_fp = TRUE}. \cr
   \code{sd_det_site} \tab Standard deviation of the site random intercept on the true-detection logit scale, when \code{random_det = TRUE}. \cr
}


\code{coef(fit, "occ")}, \code{coef(fit, "det")}, and \code{coef(fit, "fp")}
select the corresponding prefixes. \code{coef(fit, "fp")} excludes \code{fp_gap}.
\code{coef(fit)} returns posterior means of all readable draw variables,
including derived probabilities and standard deviations.
}

\section{Derived probabilities in posterior draws}{

Reference probabilities set design columns other than the intercept to
zero and random effects to zero. This means the reference factor level
under treatment contrasts, and \code{y_prev = 0} under Markov detection.
Reference values need not describe an observed site.\tabular{ll}{
   Name \tab Meaning and availability \cr
   \code{p11_ref} \tab Probability of any automated detection at an occupied reference site. In Miller, \code{plogis(det_(Intercept))}. In Chambert, \code{1 - (1 - plogis(det_(Intercept))) * (1 - plogis(fp_(Intercept)))}. Always present. \cr
   \code{p10_ref} \tab \code{plogis(fp_(Intercept))}: false-positive probability at the reference covariates, with zero site effect. Present when \code{fp} is not \code{NULL}. \cr
   \code{p10_bar} \tab Average false-positive probability over the fitted data, including fitted site effects. With site-level FP covariates, an unweighted mean over sites; with occasion-level FP covariates, a mean over unchecked automated occasions weighted by their trial counts. Confirmed occasions are excluded from this occasion average. Present when \code{fp} is not \code{NULL}. \cr
   \code{p_verified} \tab \code{plogis(verified_(Intercept))}: verified-method detection probability at reference covariates, not an average over verified occasions. Present only with verified occasions. \cr
}


When \code{fp = NULL}, false-positive probabilities are zero, but \verb{fp_*},
\code{p10_ref}, and \code{p10_bar} are omitted from the readable draws.
The Chambert observation-confirmation design requires a false-positive
process; \code{fp = NULL} is rejected for that design.
}

\section{Posterior summaries and fit diagnostics}{

\code{summary(fit)} returns \code{table}, \code{prob}, \code{diagnostics}, and \code{call}.
Its \code{table} has one row per readable variable:\tabular{ll}{
   Column \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{variable} \tab Readable parameter or derived-quantity name. \cr
   \code{mean}, \code{sd} \tab Posterior mean and standard deviation. \cr
   \code{lower}, \code{upper} \tab Equal-tailed credible interval, with width \code{prob}. \cr
   \code{rhat} \tab R-hat for this variable. \cr
   \code{ess_bulk}, \code{ess_tail} \tab Bulk and tail effective sample sizes. \cr
}


\code{fit$diagnostics} contains \code{divergences} (total divergent transitions),
\code{max_treedepth} (the number of transitions reaching the maximum depth,
not the maximum depth observed), \code{max_rhat} (largest R-hat),
\code{min_ess_bulk}, and \code{min_ess_tail} (smallest effective sample sizes).
R-hat and the effective sample sizes are summarized across the readable
variables; \code{divergences} and \code{max_treedepth} count sampler transitions.
}

\section{Predictions and site estimates}{

\code{predict(fit)} appends \code{estimate}, \code{lower}, and \code{upper} to \code{newdata}:
the posterior mean probability and equal-tailed interval for \code{process}.
With \code{summary = FALSE}, it returns a matrix with posterior draws in rows
and prediction rows in columns. These predictions use fixed effects;
site or recorder random effects are not added or integrated out.

\code{fp_effects()} returns \code{covariate}, \code{process}, \code{value} (the covariate on
the fitting scale), \code{x} (its display value after any \code{transform}),
\code{estimate}, \code{lower}, and \code{upper}, with interval width in attribute \code{prob}.

\code{fp_sites()} retains the input site columns and adds:\tabular{ll}{
   Column \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{psi}, \code{psi_lower}, \code{psi_upper} \tab Posterior mean and interval for occupancy probability given site covariates. \cr
   \code{occupied}, \code{occupied_lower}, \code{occupied_upper} \tab Posterior mean and interval of the probability of occupancy conditional on the site's observations and each parameter draw. The interval describes parameter uncertainty in that probability, not an interval for a binary state. \cr
   \code{detections} \tab Number of detections in unchecked automated occasions. Confirmed occasions are excluded. \cr
   \code{verified} \tab Whether occupancy is certain from a verified detection or a confirmed true observation (Chambert). Despite the name, it includes both sources of evidence. \cr
   \code{p10} \tab Posterior mean FP probability including the site's fitted random effect, if any. With occasion-level FP covariates, the trial-weighted average over the site's unchecked automated occasions. Present only with false positives. \cr
}


For occasion-level FP covariates, the current implementation returns
\code{p10 = 0} for a site without unchecked automated occasions; this is an
empty-data convention, not evidence for a zero false-positive rate.
}

\section{Checks and threshold sweeps}{

\code{fp_ppc()} returns \code{y} (observed detection counts by retained site),
\code{yrep} (replicated counts, draws by sites), \code{fit_obs}, \code{fit_rep}
(discrepancies per draw), \code{p_value} (the proportion with
\code{fit_rep > fit_obs}), \code{site} (retained identifiers), \code{method}, and \code{fit_stat}.

\code{fp_prior_predictive()} has columns \code{naive_occupancy} (share of all sites
with an automated detection), \code{automated_rate}, \code{verified_rate},
\code{mean_psi} (mean occupancy probability), and \code{p10_bar}. Attribute \code{observed}
holds the first three summaries for the observed data. Each row is one
simulated dataset from the priors.

\code{fp_threshold_sweep()} has columns \code{threshold}, \code{detection_rate} (share
of occasions detected), \code{parameter} (readable name), \code{mean}, \code{lower},
\code{upper}, \code{divergences}, and \code{max_rhat}. Attribute \code{prob} holds the interval
width. Named threshold selection adds \code{threshold_selection} (one row per
method) and \code{validation_metrics} (one row per candidate score):
\code{threshold}, \code{tp}, \code{fp}, \code{fn}, \code{precision}, \code{recall}, and \code{f1};
\code{threshold_selection} also has \code{method}. Here \code{fp} is a false-positive
count, not a model coefficient. See \code{\link[=fp_threshold_sweep]{fp_threshold_sweep()}} for definitions.
}

\section{Calibration and simulation}{

\code{fp_calibrate()} and \code{fp_calibrate_fit()} return \code{cell}, \code{rep}, \code{seed},
\code{model}, \code{parameter}, \code{truth}, \code{mean}, \code{lower}, \code{upper}, \code{covered},
\code{divergences}, \code{max_rhat}, \code{ok}, and \code{seconds}; \code{fp_calibrate()} adds the
columns of its \code{design}.
\code{ok} records whether fitting returned a result, not convergence.
\code{summary()} groups by cell, model and parameter and returns the design
columns plus \code{n} (successful fits), \code{failed}, \code{coverage}, \code{mc_se}
(Monte Carlo standard error of coverage), \code{bias}, \code{rmse}, and
\code{prop_divergent} (fraction of successful fits with divergences).
\code{fp_verification_curve()} returns the calibration object invisibly;
its printed curve selects \code{model}, \code{frac_verified}, \code{coverage},
\code{mc_se}, and \code{bias} for \code{occ_x}.

\code{fp_sbc()} returns \code{rep}, \code{parameter}, \code{truth}, \code{rank} (number of
retained draws below the true value), \code{divergences}, and \code{ok}, with
attribute \code{ndraws}. Its \code{summary()} returns \code{parameter}, \code{n}, \code{mean_rank}
(mean rank divided by \code{ndraws}), \code{p_uniform} (chi-squared rank-histogram
test p-value), and \code{divergent} (fraction with divergences); attribute
\code{failed} counts failed replicates. In SBC only, parameter \code{log_lik} is
the total log likelihood, rather than a site's contribution.

\code{simulate_fp()} returns \code{data} and \code{truth}. The latter records the
generating arguments (\code{psi}, \code{b1}, \code{p11}, \code{p10}, \code{r11}, \code{sigma_p10},
\code{p10_conc}, \code{conf_effect}, \code{conf_cor}, \code{a_bio}, \code{a_aci}, \code{sd_recorder},
\code{frac_confirmed}), \code{b0} (occupancy intercept), \code{regime},
\code{conf_cor_realized}, \code{n_verified_sites}, \code{z} (binary state by site),
\code{p10_site}, and \code{p10_marginal} (mean of \code{p10_site}).
\code{simulate(fit)} returns a list of datasets, each with \code{data} and \code{truth};
its truth fields are \code{pars} (named generating coefficients), \code{z},
\code{p10_site}, and \code{p10_bar}.
}

\section{Readable draws and raw Stan names}{

The fitted object contains \code{draws}, \code{fit} (the backend fit), \code{data},
\code{formulas}, \code{markov}, \code{random_recorder}, \code{random_fp}, \code{random_det}, \code{priors},
\code{sampler}, \code{diagnostics}, \code{call}, \code{design}, and \code{stan_data}.
\code{sampler} records \code{chains}, \code{parallel_chains}, \code{threads_per_chain},
\code{iter_warmup}, \code{iter_sampling}, and \code{adapt_delta}.

\code{as_draws_df(fit)} returns the readable variables above, with posterior
metadata \code{.chain}, \code{.iteration}, and \code{.draw}. The backend's
\code{fit$fit$draws()} also exposes raw Stan names. Current mappings are:\tabular{ll}{
   Stan name \tab Readable name \cr
   \code{beta[k]} \tab \verb{occ_<term>} \cr
   \code{alpha[k]} \tab \verb{det_<term>} \cr
   \code{gamma0}, \code{gamma_fp[k]} \tab \code{fp_(Intercept)}, \verb{fp_<term>} \cr
   \code{delta0}, \code{delta_s[k]} \tab \code{verified_(Intercept)}, \verb{verified_<term>} \cr
   \code{r11} \tab \code{p_verified} \cr
   \code{sigma_u}, \code{sigma_fp[1]}, \code{sigma_det[1]} \tab \code{sd_recorder}, \code{sd_fp_site}, \code{sd_det_site} \cr
}


\code{fp_gap}, \code{p11_ref}, \code{p10_ref}, and \code{p10_bar} keep their names.
\code{log_lik[i]} is the state-marginalized log likelihood of site \code{i}, in
input site-table order; \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} uses sites as its pointwise units.
It is available from \code{fit$fit$draws("log_lik")}, not the readable draws.
With a fitted site random effect, state marginalization does not also
integrate that effect out; \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} does, by quadrature (its argument
\code{integrate}). Raw random effects (\code{u}, \code{u_raw}, \code{e_raw}, \code{d_raw})
and sampler variable \code{lp__} are backend outputs, not additional public
coefficient names. \code{design} and \code{stan_data} describe internal encodings;
their field names and raw Stan names are implementation details.
}

